<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sextus Pompey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 67–35 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Sextus Pompeius Magnus Pius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sextus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> was a Roman general, the younger son of Pompey the Great. After the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> he built a naval power based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, sheltering opponents of the triumvirs and cutting off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s grain supply. Octavian's admiral Agrippa defeated him at Naulochus in 36 BCE, and he was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> in 35 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sextus-pompey.jpg" alt="Sextus Pompey" width="320" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dimka Totskiy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aureus_Sextus_Pompeius_42BC_Goldberg_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
